''Black Lightning'' has received generally positive reviews from professional critics. On the [[review aggregation]] website [[Rotten Tomatoes]], the series holds a 95% approval rating.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/black_lightning/|title=Black Lightning (2018 - )|website=[[Rotten Tomatoes]]|publisher=[[Fandango Media]]|access-date=November 3, 2019|archive-date=November 13, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191113164412/https://www.rottentomatoes.com/tv/black_lightning|url-status=live}}</ref> The first season debuted to critical acclaim.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.paleycenter.org/2019-black-lightning/|title=An Electrifying Black Lightning Conversation on Good vs. Evil|website=[[Paley Center for Media]]|date=February 11, 2018|access-date=June 15, 2019|archive-date=June 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612120529/https://www.paleycenter.org/events/2019-black-lightning/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.themarysue.com/black-lightning-grittier-feels/|title=New Black Lightning Poster Gives Hints of a Grittier Seasons|website=[[The Mary Sue]]|last=Weekes|first=Princess|date=August 29, 2018|access-date=June 15, 2019|archive-date=November 24, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181124154508/https://www.themarysue.com/black-lightning-grittier-feels/amp/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.colorlines.com/articles/cw-renews-black-lightning|title=The CW Renews 'Black Lightning'|website=Color Lines|last=Rao|first=Sameer|date=April 3, 2018|access-date=June 15, 2019|archive-date=June 16, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190616055452/https://www.colorlines.com/articles/cw-renews-black-lightning|url-status=live}}</ref>

Reviewing for ''[[Entertainment Weekly]]'', Dana Schwartz gave the series premiere an "A−" grade, writing, "The Pierce family dynamic is so compelling that even if Jefferson Pierce had no powers beyond great motivational speeches, this show would be a worthwhile watch. ''Black Lightning'' balances humor with all-too-necessary social commentary [...] to make a refreshing addition to the superhero TV pantheon."<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/tv/2018/01/16/black-lightning-crackles-with-electricity-and-relevance-ew-review/|title=Black Lightning crackles with electricity and relevance: EW review|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|last=Schwartz|first=Dana|date=January 16, 2018|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=June 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604051256/https://ew.com/tv/2018/01/16/black-lightning-crackles-with-electricity-and-relevance-ew-review/|url-status=live}}</ref> Reviewing the first two episodes of the season, Daniel Fienberg of ''[[The Hollywood Reporter]]'' commended the series for its "solid action scenes, bass-pumping soundtrack, stylish treatment of Black Lightning's sizzling powers and character pragmatism." He praised the series' ability to "stand alone in welcome ways" and described the premiere episode as "smart and relevant and full of an attitude that's all its own."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/review/black-lightning-review-1074361|title='Black Lightning': TV Review|website=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]|last=Fienberg|first=Daniel|date=January 15, 2018|access-date=May 22, 2019|archive-date=June 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604051255/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/review/black-lightning-review-1074361|url-status=live}}</ref> Reviewing the first four episodes of the series, ''[[The Atlantic]]''{{'}}s Pilot Viruet wrote, "There's an authenticity to the series—it's neither too pulpy nor too preachy—that's heightened by the strong performances from its predominantly Black cast, particularly from [Cress] Williams, who anchors the show's many conflicts."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2018/02/black-lightning-the-cw-review/552947/|title=In Black Lightning, There's No Right Way to Fix a City|website=[[The Atlantic]]|last=Viruet|first=Pilot|date=February 13, 2018|access-date=May 22, 2019|archive-date=June 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604051306/https://www.theatlantic.com/entertainment/archive/2018/02/black-lightning-the-cw-review/552947/|url-status=live}}</ref> Nafessa Williams' portrayal of Anissa Pierce, Black Lightning's eldest daughter who becomes the superhero Thunder, also drew attention and praise from critics. As the first Black lesbian superhero on television, critics applauded the character's social activism and action scenes, as well as for being a "complex" and "fully realized" character outside of her superhero identity.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.inverse.com/article/40691-black-lightning-lesbian-superhero-thunder|title=Why 'Black Lightning' Could Create the Future for Lesbian Superheroes|website=[[Inverse (website)|Inverse]]|last=Yohannes|first=Alamin|date=January 30, 2018|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=June 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604051306/https://www.inverse.com/article/40691-black-lightning-lesbian-superhero-thunder|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/black-lightning-anissa-jennifer-pierce-superheroes|title=Daughters of Lightning: How Anissa and Jennifer Pierce Are Setting the Standard for Black Women Superheroes|website=[[Syfy Wire]]|last=Yohannes|first=Alamin|date=February 2, 2018|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=June 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604051256/https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/black-lightning-anissa-jennifer-pierce-superheroes|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://blackgirlnerds.com/anissa-pierce-queer-representation/|title=I'm Already Here: Anissa Pierce & Black Lightning's Queer Representation|website=Black Girl Nerds|last=Lawrence|first=Briana|date=January 25, 2018|access-date=June 3, 2019|archive-date=June 4, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190604051300/https://blackgirlnerds.com/anissa-pierce-queer-representation/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.advocate.com/television/2019/6/06/black-lightnings-nafessa-williams-force-nature%3famp|title=Black Lightning's Nafessa Williams Is a Force of Nature|website=[[Advocate.com]]|last=Anderson-Minshall|first=Diane|date=June 6, 2019|access-date=June 15, 2019|archive-date=June 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190607093608/https://www.advocate.com/television/2019/6/06/black-lightnings-nafessa-williams-force-nature?amp|url-status=live}}</ref> Reviewing for ''[[The A.V. Club]]'', Kyle Fowle gave the finale an "A" grade, writing that the episode was "tonally bold, easily moving between being funny and politically poignant," while also setting up "plenty of intrigue" for next season. Fowle concluded that the episode was a "confident, compelling, moving end to a season that boasted all those same qualities throughout."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.avclub.com/black-lightning-closes-out-a-strong-debut-season-with-a-1825335833|title=Black Lightning closes out a strong debut season with a perfect, stirring finale|website=[[The A.V. Club]]|last=Fowle|first=Kyle|date=April 17, 2018|access-date=May 22, 2019|archive-date=July 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190707185002/https://tv.avclub.com/black-lightning-closes-out-a-strong-debut-season-with-a-1825335833|url-status=live}}</ref> Jesse Schedeen of ''[[IGN]]'' noted that the series' reduced episode count, focus on older superheroes and social issues, limited cast, and preservation of the main villain at the end of the season positively distinguished the series from other superhero programming.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://m.ign.com/articles/2018/04/27/5-things-the-arrowverse-can-learn-from-black-lightning|title=5 Things the Arrowverse Can Learn From Black Lightning|website=[[IGN]]|last=Schedeen|first=Jesse|date=April 27, 2018|access-date=May 23, 2019|archive-date=June 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612120525/https://www.ign.com/articles/2018/04/27/5-things-the-arrowverse-can-learn-from-black-lightning|url-status=live}}</ref>

''The A.V. Club''{{'}}s Fowle gave the second-season premiere an "A−" grade, adding, "One of the reasons the first season was so compelling was that the show made the political personal. That's once again evident here, as there's a wonderful sense of the larger community throughout the premiere."<ref name="Fowle">{{cite web|url=https://www.avclub.com/black-lightning-is-back-and-its-bleaker-and-better-tha-1829640658|title=Black Lightning is back, and it's bleaker and better than ever|website=[[The A.V. Club]]|last=Fowle|first=Kyle|date=October 9, 2018|access-date=October 27, 2018|archive-date=October 28, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181028073857/https://tv.avclub.com/black-lightning-is-back-and-its-bleaker-and-better-tha-1829640658|url-status=live}}</ref> Kayti Burt of ''[[Den of Geek!]]'' gave the episode a rating of 3.5 stars out of 5. She explained, "There's a lack of thematic cohesiveness in this season premiere that is probably a result of trying to reintroduce so many characters and storylines at once, but the energy and potential for a strong second season is definitely there. ''Black Lightning'' continues to be one of the best, most important shows on television."<ref name="Burt">{{cite web|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/tv/black-lightning-season-2-episode-1-review/|title=Black Lightning Season 2 Episode 1 Review — The Book of Consequences: Chapter One: Rise of the Green Light Babies|website=[[Den of Geek!]]|last=Burt|first=Kayti|date=October 11, 2018|access-date=October 17, 2018|archive-date=October 17, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181017170906/http://www.denofgeek.com/us/tv/black-lightning/276656/black-lightning-season-2-episode-1-review|url-status=live}}</ref> In her review for ''[[Collider (website)|Collider]]'', Allison Keene gave the season's early storylines 4 stars out of 5, writing, "''Black Lightning'' is not without its own flaws, including occasionally disjointed or abrupt storytelling, but its clear desire to educate and inspire through compelling family drama continues to make it a show of consequence."<ref name="Keene">{{cite web|url=https://collider.com/black-lightning-season-2-review/#images|title='Black Lightning' Season 2 Review: Family, Truth, and Consequences|website=[[Collider (website)|Collider]]|last=Keene|first=Allison|date=October 8, 2018|access-date=March 23, 2019|archive-date=March 22, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190322152039/http://collider.com/black-lightning-season-2-review/#images|url-status=live}}</ref> Burt and ''[[Syfy Wire]]'' contributor Stephanie Williams praised the season for its efforts to de-stigmatize mental-health care, especially for Black women. Burt called Jennifer's therapy storyline "a powerful, vital representation of mental-health issues—not only within the superhero genre, but anywhere on TV."<ref name="BurtAnalysis">{{cite web|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/tv/black-lightnings-depiction-of-superhero-mental-illness-is-so-important/|title=Black Lightning's Depiction of Superhero Mental Illness is So Important|website=[[Den of Geek!]]|last=Burt|first=Kayti|date=October 25, 2018|access-date=March 30, 2019|archive-date=July 1, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190701000303/https://www.denofgeek.com/us/tv/black-lightning/277191/black-lightnings-depiction-of-superhero-mental-illness-is-so-important|url-status=live}}</ref> Again reviewing for ''The A.V. Club'', Fowle gave the season finale a "C+" grade. He named Jennifer's journey as "undoubtedly" the best part of the season, but felt that the second season as a whole "struggled to reach the heights of its first season" and that "storylines were introduced with full force before petering out,[...] stumbling towards lackluster conclusions."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.avclub.com/freeland-burns-but-black-lightning-loses-some-of-its-f-1833387381|title=Freeland burns, but Black Lightning loses some of its fire|website=[[The A.V. Club]]|last=Fowle|first=Kyle|date=March 18, 2019|access-date=March 23, 2019|archive-date=March 19, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190319122326/https://tv.avclub.com/freeland-burns-but-black-lightning-loses-some-of-its-f-1833387381|url-status=live}}</ref> Dan Ashby of ''Cultured Vultures'' gave the whole season a rating of 7.5 out of 10, writing, "Season two of ''Black Lightning'' has some interesting storylines, a number of good fight scenes, a catchy soundtrack, and some important messages. Unfortunately, it all seems slightly uncoordinated and messy in its execution."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://culturedvultures.com/black-lightning-season-2-review-all-the-right-pieces-but-faulty-execution/|title=Black Lightning Season 2 Review – All The Right Pieces, But Faulty Execution|website=Cultured Vultures|last=Ashby|first=Dan|date=April 5, 2019|access-date=April 7, 2019|archive-date=June 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612120543/https://culturedvultures.com/black-lightning-season-2-review-all-the-right-pieces-but-faulty-execution/|url-status=live}}</ref> In a more positive account from ''Entertainment Weekly'', Christian Holub gave the season finale a "B" grade, adding, "Season 2's penultimate installment was all over the place, but tonight's season finale did a good job of bringing enough of the threads together that I'm now satisfied to wait for season 3."<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-2-finale/|title=Black Lightning season finale recap: Jennifer suits up|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|last=Holub|first=Christian|date=March 18, 2019|access-date=March 30, 2019|archive-date=March 30, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190330233309/https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-2-finale/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Reviewing the third-season premiere for ''Entertainment Weekly'', Holub gave the episode a "B+" grade, writing, "''Black Lightning'' has been able to do a really good job of showcasing real-life racial dynamics and structural inequities through its comic-flavored storytelling. And in season 3, the show is aiming directly at our real-life crisis of family separation and putting kids in cages. [...] After a bit of a sophomore slump, I'm excited to see where ''Black Lightning'' goes from here."<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-3-premiere/|title=Black Lightning season premiere recap: Freeland puts kids in cages too|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|last=Holub|first=Christian|date=October 8, 2019|access-date=October 17, 2019|archive-date=October 17, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191017220412/https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-3-premiere/|url-status=live}}</ref> Nicole Hill of ''Den of Geek!'' assigned the premiere episode a rating of 4 out of 5. She opined that, "''Black Lightning'' does a good job of playing with these different concepts of power and showing how they interact, overlap, and cancel each other out. [...] I hope this season maintains focus and doesn't find itself branching out too far away from its central conflicts. If the following episodes consistently operate at this level, this will be an exciting season to watch."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.denofgeek.com/us/tv/black-lightning/283699/black-lightning-season-3-episode-1-review-the-book-of-occupation-chapter-one-the-birth-of-blackbird|title=Black Lightning Season 3 Episode 1 Review: The Book of Occupation: Chapter One: The Birth of Blackbird|website=[[Den of Geek!]]|last=Hill|first=Nicole|date=October 7, 2019|access-date=October 17, 2019|archive-date=October 18, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191018003253/https://www.denofgeek.com/us/tv/black-lightning/283699/black-lightning-season-3-episode-1-review-the-book-of-occupation-chapter-one-the-birth-of-blackbird|url-status=dead}}</ref> ''The A.V. Club''{{'}}s Fowle gave the premiere a "B+" grade and added that, "The first episode of the third season is just as politically charged as previous episodes, but this time around, things feel more relevant, more attuned to this specific time and place. ''Black Lightning'' has nodded towards real-life events before, but this premiere, centered around kids being rounded up, separated from their families, and locked in detention camps with no idea of when they'll get out or what they did wrong, feels particularly pointed."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.avclub.com/black-lightning-adapts-to-the-current-political-moment-1838860250|title=Black Lightning adapts to the current political moment|website=[[The A.V. Club]]|last=Fowle|first=Kyle|date=October 7, 2019|access-date=October 17, 2019|archive-date=October 18, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191018003249/https://tv.avclub.com/black-lightning-adapts-to-the-current-political-moment-1838860250|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Analysis===
Tai Gooden of ''Syfy Wire'' wrote, "The usage of religion, specifically Christianity, in ''Black Lightning'' strikes a fascinating balance between the science-centered A.S.A. organization and the fantasy element of metahumans whose powers essentially make them gods among men." Gooden pointed out that each episode title contains religious references, particularly the second season's multi-episode chapters named after themes frequently explored in religious texts, such as consequences, blood, and rebellion. Additionally, she highlighted the role of Reverend Holt in the series, whose sermons "are often interspersed with scenes that either complement or counteract a character's current struggle with faith and making the right decisions." She compared Khalil's arc to that of one who has lost their faith, and likened Tobias to a Devil that offers temptations. "The primary characters and community members tackle their personal and collective plights with bold protests, prayers, vigilante justice, and rogue missions, all of which are threaded together through overt and subtle religious references."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/religion-in-black-lightning-a-journey-of-rage-redemption-and-righteousness|title=Religion in Black Lightning: A Journey of Rage, Redemption, and Righteousness|website=[[Syfy Wire]]|last=Gooden|first=Tai|date=April 29, 2019|access-date=June 3, 2019|archive-date=April 30, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190430153857/https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/religion-in-black-lightning-a-journey-of-rage-redemption-and-righteousness|url-status=live}}</ref>

Several journalists drew parallels between some of the series's storylines, particularly those involving [[police brutality]] and [[institutional racism]], and events that have taken place in real life.<ref name="Fowle" /><ref name="Burt" /><ref name="Keene" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://m.ign.com/articles/2018/10/10/black-lightning-season-2-premiere-review-the-book-of-consequences-chapter-one-rise-of-the-green-light-babies|title=Black Lightning Season 2 Premiere Review: "The Book of Consequences - Chapter One: Rise of the Green Light Babies"|website=[[IGN]]|last=Waldron|first=Carl|date=October 10, 2018|access-date=October 17, 2018|archive-date=June 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612120526/https://www.ign.com/articles/2018/10/10/black-lightning-season-2-premiere-review-the-book-of-consequences-chapter-one-rise-of-the-green-light-babies|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-2-episode-2/|title=Black Lightning recap: Confronting those deep, dark truths|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|last=Holub|first=Christian|date=October 16, 2018|access-date=April 7, 2019|archive-date=April 8, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190408001056/https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-2-episode-2/|url-status=live}}</ref> Christina Radish of ''Collider'' and Charles Pulliam-Moore of ''[[io9]]'' felt that the character Issa Williams, a black teen who is held in a fatal chokehold by police as bystanders record the incident and plead for his life, was "reminiscent" of the 2014 [[death of Eric Garner]]. Pulliam-Moore also noted that, "The families of Freeland who are fighting to get their children back [from government custody] clearly call to mind all of the undocumented children who've been [[Trump administration family separation policy|separated from their families]], are being held in detention centers, and may very well never see their loved ones again."<ref name="Collider" /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://io9.gizmodo.com/black-lightnings-explosive-season-2-premiere-came-out-s-1829657497|title=Black Lightning's Explosive Season 2 Premiere Came Out Swinging|website=[[Gizmodo]]|last=Pulliam-Moore|first=Charles|date=October 10, 2018|access-date=October 30, 2018|archive-date=October 31, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181031133022/https://io9.gizmodo.com/black-lightnings-explosive-season-2-premiere-came-out-s-1829657497|url-status=live}}</ref> Additionally, ''Entertainment Weekly''{{'}}s Christian Holub compared Anissa's [[Robin Hood]]-esque crime-fighting philosophy and alternative costume, a black hoodie and mouth cover, to the philosophies and costumes of [[Antifa (United States)|Antifa]] members.<ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-premiere-recap-freeland-changing/|title=Black Lightning season premiere recap: Freeland is changing|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|last=Holub|first=Christian|date=October 9, 2018|access-date=April 7, 2019|archive-date=April 8, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190408001053/https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-premiere-recap-freeland-changing/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite magazine|url=https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-2-episode-3/|title=Black Lightning recap: Meet the new boss|magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]]|last=Holub|first=Christian|date=October 23, 2018|access-date=April 7, 2019|archive-date=April 7, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190407102416/https://ew.com/recap/black-lightning-season-2-episode-3/|url-status=live}}</ref>

While applauding Jennifer's therapy arc and the series's portrayal of mental health care, Kayti Burt of ''Den of Geek!'' cited the scene in the second-season premiere episode in which Jefferson brushes off Lynn's suggestion that he should see a therapist as an example of double standards regarding age and gender. She commented, "The depiction of Jennifer's mental health treatment is so important, but, as a teen girl, Jennifer is allowed a degree of emotional vulnerability that [[Toxic masculinity|adult men are not]]." Burt felt that this apparent point of hypocrisy intentionally served as a subject for the story to reflect upon. She said, "''Black Lightning'' isn't ignorant to the many reasons for avoiding shows of emotional vulnerability, of the potential consequences of asking for help in a world that so often equates emotional vulnerability with weakness. It just also chooses to tell the other side of that story: the potential consequences of not acknowledging and actively working on your emotional and mental health issues."<ref name="BurtAnalysis" />

